<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Otto I, Duke of Bavaria</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 1117–<a href="/years/1183">1183</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/12th-century">12th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc"><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/otto-i">Otto I</a> of Wittelsbach was a Bavarian count palatine and a loyal follower of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/frederick-barbarossa">Frederick Barbarossa</a>, whom he served on campaigns in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a>. When Barbarossa deposed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/henry-the-lion">Henry the Lion</a> in 1180, he gave the reduced Duchy of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bavaria">Bavaria</a> to Otto, founding the rule of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/house-of-wittelsbach">House of Wittelsbach</a>, which lasted in Bavaria until 1918. Otto died in 1183.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/otto-i-duke-of-bavaria.jpg" alt="Otto I, Duke of Bavaria" width="320" height="462" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown creator · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Otto_Magnus_Wittelspach.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>…a stem duchy of the Empire it passed between families: the Ottonian kings gave it to relatives, the Welfs held it from 1070 with interruptions, and in 1180 Frederick Barbarossa deposed Henry the Lion and granted the duchy to <b>Otto of Wittelsbach</b>, whose family ruled Bavaria until 1918.</blockquote><a href="/empire/duchy-of-bavaria">Duchy of Bavaria · Governance &amp; Law</a></li><li><blockquote>Its Count Walram II and his brother <b>Otto I</b> jointly ruled it in the mid 13th century.</blockquote><a href="/state/county-of-nassau">County of Nassau</a></li><li><blockquote>In 953 <b>Otto I</b> made his brother Bruno archbishop and also duke of Lotharingia, founding the temporal power of the see.</blockquote><a href="/state/electorate-of-cologne">Electorate of Cologne</a></li><li><blockquote>Around 960 the canoness Hrotsvitha wrote Latin plays, legends and verse histories of <b>Otto I</b> there.</blockquote><a href="/state/imperial-abbey-of-gandersheim">Imperial Abbey of Gandersheim</a></li><li><blockquote>In 967 <b>Otto I</b> confirmed Aleramo, a nobleman of Frankish descent, as margrave of lands in the hills south of the Po between Turin and Genoa, later called Montferrat.</blockquote><a href="/state/march-of-montferrat">March of Montferrat</a></li><li><blockquote>In 952 <b>Otto I</b> forced Berengar II, King of Italy, to cede Verona, Friuli, Aquileia and Istria.</blockquote><a href="/state/march-of-verona">March of Verona</a></li></ul></section>
